==English==
{{wikipedia|Allahu Akbar}}

===Alternative forms===
[[Image:FlagofWaziristan.png|thumb|{{l|ar|الله أكبر}}]]
* [[Allah akbar]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ar|en}} {{term|الله أكبر|lang=ar|tr=allāhu akbar}}, from nominative form of {{term|الله||God|lang=ar|tr=’allāh}} + {{term|أكبر|lang=ar|tr=’ákbar}}, [[elative]] form of {{term|كبير|lang=ar|tr=kabīr||great}}.

===Pronunciation===
*{{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌaləhuː ˈakbɑː/|/əˌlɑːəhuː ˈakbɑː/}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interjection}}

# {{context|Islam|lang=en}} "[[God]] is most [[great]]"; the [[Muslim]] [[takbir]], or liturgical [[proclamation]], used as a common form of thanksgiving, expression of faith or [[war cry]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#*'''1742''', James Fraser, ''The History of Nadir Shah'', London 1742, p. 126n:
#*:''Tokbîr'' is repeating three times these Words, {{lang|ar|الله أكبر}} '''''Allah Akbar''''', which is to say, GOD ''is Greatest'', before the ''Kelmah'' abovementioned.
#*'''2004''', Yuri Bagrov, ''The Guardian'', 22 Jun 2004:
#*:The official said it was not immediately clear who the attackers were, but said some of them were shouting "'''Allahu Akbar'''" - a frequent cry of Chechnya's separatist rebels.

